        <body>
            <div xml:id="d232-a10">

                <pb facs="0588" n="566" sameAs="frus:frus1872p2v2/pg566"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 10 in No. 97.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">General Schenck to Earl Granville.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-06-08"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 8,
                        1872</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Lord</hi>: I received at seven o’clock last
                    evening your note of yesterday referring to what Sir Edward Thornton has stated
                    to you in regard to Mr. Fish’s objection to the word “belligerent,” and
                    suggesting a modification of language to obviate that objection.</p>
                <p>I transmitted the full text of your note by telegraph to Mr. Fish immediately. At
                    the same time I informed him that I am giving your Lordship no ground for
                    believing that the Government of the United States will be able now to assent to
                    any change of the rule as expressed by the Senate amendment.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, with the highest consideration, your Lordship’s most
                    obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>ROBT. C. SCHENCK.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
